- name: Normalizes but does not hash personal data when hashing is switched off
  code: |-
    setEventData(ga4Purchase);
    const settings = withPlatform('meta');
    settings.hashUserData = false;
    const result = runCode(settings);
    assertThat(result.user_data.em).isEqualTo('test@example.com');
- name: Hashes Google Ads phone in E.164 format, distinct from Meta's digits-only format
  code: |-
    setEventData(ga4Purchase);
    const metaResult = runCode(withPlatform('meta'));
    const adsResult = runCode(withPlatform('google_ads'));
    assertThat(adsResult.user_data.phone_number.length).isEqualTo(64);
    assertThat(adsResult.user_data.phone_number).isNotEqualTo(metaResult.user_data.ph);
- name: Maps a custom schema to the Snapchat structure
  code: |-
    setEventData(customSchema);
    const result = runCode(withPlatform('snapchat'));
    assertThat(result.event_type).isEqualTo('PURCHASE');
    assertThat(result.transaction_id).isEqualTo('INV-2026-88');
    assertThat(result.currency).isEqualTo('AED');
    assertThat(result.item_ids).isEqualTo(['ABC']);
- name: Returns the event name only when asked for it
  code: |-
    setEventData(ga4Purchase);
    const settings = withPlatform('tiktok');
    settings.outputMode = 'event';
    assertThat(runCode(settings)).isEqualTo('CompletePayment');
- name: Invents nothing when the event data is empty
  code: |-
    setEventData({});
    const result = runCode(withPlatform('ga4'));
    assertThat(result.params.value).isUndefined();
    assertThat(result.params.items).isUndefined();
- name: Coerces a numeric item_id to a string in the output
  code: |-
    setEventData({ event_name: 'purchase', items: [{ item_id: 12345, price: 1, quantity: 1 }] });
    const result = runCode(withPlatform('meta'));
    assertThat(result.custom_data.content_ids[0]).isEqualTo('12345');
setup: |-
  const ga4Purchase = {
    event_name: 'purchase',
    transaction_id: 'T-12345',
    value: 249.9,
    currency: 'USD',
    items: [
      { item_id: 'SKU-1', item_name: 'Blue Shirt', price: 99.95, quantity: 2 },
      { item_id: 'SKU-2', item_name: 'Cap', price: 50, quantity: 1 }
    ],
    user_data: { email_address: 'Test@Example.com', phone_number: '+1 415 555 2671' }
  };

  const customSchema = {
    event_name: 'OrderCompleted',
    order: {
      orderNumber: 'INV-2026-88',
      total: 560.4,
      currencyCode: 'AED',
      lineItems: [{ productId: 'ABC', productName: 'Desk Lamp', unitPrice: 280.2, qty: 2 }]
    }
  };

  let eventData = {};
  const setEventData = (value) => {
    eventData = value;
  };

  mock('getAllEventData', () => eventData);

  const withPlatform = (platform) => {
    return {
      sourceMode: 'auto',
      platform: platform,
      outputMode: 'object',
      missingMode: 'omit',
      calculateValue: false,
      hashUserData: true,
      actionSource: 'website',
      eventConversionType: 'WEB'
    };
  };

___NOTES___

Universal Data Layer Converter (Server) by MD Niamul

Turns server container event data (or a chosen variable) into the exact
structure a marketing platform expects, so the same source data can feed
GA4, Google Ads, Meta, TikTok, LinkedIn, Snapchat, Pinterest, Microsoft Ads
and more without a Custom JavaScript variable per platform.

No value is ever invented. A field that is not present in the source stays
empty, or is returned as the placeholder text you configure.

This is the SERVER (sGTM) variant. It reads server-side event data via
getAllEventData() and can hash PII (email, phone, name, address) with
SHA-256 before it leaves your server container, per the platform's required
format. For the client-side (Web container) variant of this template, which
never hashes data in the browser, see "Universal Data Layer Converter by
MD Niamul".

Phone numbers must already include their country code in the source data;
this template normalizes format (E.164 with "+" for Google Ads and
Microsoft/Bing UET, digits-only for Meta/TikTok/Snapchat/Pinterest/etc.) but
never invents a country code.

Why this template requests the "Read event data" permission with
eventDataAccess "any": in "Auto" source mode it reads the incoming event's
entire data object via getAllEventData(), because it must accept whatever
event structure your server container receives (GA4-style, Universal
Analytics-style, or a fully custom schema) without knowing its shape in
advance — that is the template's whole purpose. It does not forward,
log outside the debug console, or store this data anywhere; it only maps
recognized fields into the output you request.


